patch — the sound graph

  patch                        every node, its ports, and the links
  patch tone:out hda:out       link a source into a sink
  patch -u tone:out hda:out    part them again
  patch default hda:out        where sound goes when nobody says
  patch vol music:out 50       one port's own volume
  patch mute music:out         toggle one port's silence

Everything that makes or takes sound is a node with ports. A source
port produces frames, a sink port consumes them, and a link joins one
of each. The hardware is a node like any other: its `out` is the way
out of the machine and its `in` is the microphone.

Ports are named `node:port`, or just the node when it has one port in
the direction wanted. Two sources into one sink are mixed; one source
into two sinks is copied to both. Neither is a setting, both are what
the shape of the graph means.

The defaults point at the hardware until something points them
elsewhere, which is what lets a program play without naming anything
and a workflow rearrange everything without the program knowing.
